 ## 7. The converse question and the extent of the result
 
-A correct positive class for the converse is supplied by the known complete-splitting theorem: let A be simple of CM type and let p split completely in the Galois closure of End^0(A). For a reduction at p, the all-powers Hodge Lefschetz property is equivalent to the all-powers Tate Lefschetz property. This is Sugiyama, arXiv:1301.4005, Theorem 0.1(2)(b). In its proof the decomposition group is trivial, the CM and Frobenius character conditions coincide, and the Lefschetz groups agree.
+For the literal existential converse, take the class of all CM elliptic curves over Qbar. Every power of such a curve already has all Hodge classes generated by divisors: over a splitting coefficient field the two conjugate CM characters must occur with equal multiplicities in an invariant exterior monomial, so that monomial factors into degree-two invariants. The Lefschetz (1,1) theorem and scalar descent identify these with divisor products. Thus, for every member of this class and every reduction, the implication from the reduction property to the characteristic-zero property holds. This is an elementary affirmative class, not a novelty claim.
+
+A stronger bidirectional result for specified pairs (A, prime) is supplied by the known complete-splitting theorem: let A be simple of CM type and let p split completely in the Galois closure of End^0(A). For a reduction at p, the all-powers Hodge Lefschetz property is equivalent to the all-powers Tate Lefschetz property. This is Sugiyama, arXiv:1301.4005, Theorem 0.1(2)(b). In its proof the decomposition group is trivial, the CM and Frobenius character conditions coincide, and the Lefschetz groups agree.
 
 The OWR contribution, Theorem 1(2), phrases its hypothesis in terms of an unramified degree-one prime of the reflex field. The accessible preprint instead uses the Galois closure of the endomorphism field. This packet uses only the stronger, directly checked complete-splitting hypothesis. It does not silently promote one version to the other.
 
