 Its cited input is the Shimura-Taniyama slope formula, Chai-Conrad-Oort, Proposition 2.1.4.2. That formula gives an **average of the CM type on local embedding fibers**. The next inference, from the average formula to equality of the dimensions of the two orbit spans, is the precise inference under challenge. In our matrices the unaveraged span has dimension 8 and the averaged span dimension 4. The full Frobenius torus has dimension 5 and the CM Mumford-Tate torus dimension 9; Dodson's convention includes the weight dimension, so there is an additional one-dimensional convention to track, but it does not explain 9 versus 5. At a completely split prime averaging does nothing, so the asserted implication does hold in that restricted setting.
 
